$\Delta G_R$ is a complex, two-dimensional function that depends on pressure and temperature. These two variables are referred to as the external, experimental conditions of the reaction and can be controlled during an experiment. Before we dive deeper into 2D and 3D plots, let us address the meaning of pressure conditions. In order to start a reaction, we will have to mix the reactants and sometimes the products in a certain mixture. As reactants and products tend to be gases, that would mean we need certain partial pressures of reactant and products. This set of pressures is referred to as the \emph{pressure conditions}. Among the many different pressure conditions, the standard pressure conditions and the equilibrium pressure conditions are two important pressure conditions.

On one hand, at \emph{standard pressure conditions} all reactant and product pressures are 1atm, the reference pressure. At these conditions $Q_c$ is one and Log$Q_c$ is zero (remember Log(1)=0). In these conditions $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ equals to $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$, the reference $\Delta G$ value. Standard conditions represent a single point on the Gibbs free energy two-dimensional plot (Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines}, star mark). Under these conditions, $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ can be either positive or negative, depending on the tabulated standard Gibbs free energy values. On the other hand, in \emph{equilibrium pressure conditions}, all reactant and product pressures correspond to their corresponding equilibrium value and therefore $Q_c$, the pressure ratio, equals $K_c$, the real equilibrium constant. Under these pressure conditions, the reaction has reached equilibrium. In these conditions, we also have that $\Delta G_R$ will be zero. The equilibrium pressure conditions do not represent a point but an entire line in the $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$-3D plot. Along this line, $\Delta G_R$=0, giving a set of temperature and pressure conditions. This line is called the equilibrium isoline. 